// item 391 / issue #106: the ACCEPTING side of the two G6 shadowing refusals
// (`selfhost/lower.rvl`'s `csh_refusal` and `mth_rebind`, the reference's
// `_refuse_callable_shadowing` and `_check_rebind`). Both refusals were ported
// into a gate that had never read either rule, and both are one careless
// widening away from refusing programs the reference admits — a name that
// merely coincides with a module callable, and a method-local that merely
// coincides with another method's. This corpus had no example of either shape,
// so nothing would have caught that widening: the census would have reported a
// false-rejection, and the byte-agreement projections would have reported
// nothing at all, because they only compare documents that still compile.
//
// Every declaration below therefore has to keep COMPILING and keep emitting the
// same bytes on both sides. The comment on each says which arm of which rule it
// stands in front of.

// `_refuse_callable_shadowing` fires on the CALL position only. Here `name` is
// a module fn and also a parameter and a local, and the coincidence is all it
// is: nothing calls the bare name, so both readings agree and the program is
// legal. This is the shape the reference's own note cites
// (backends/wasm/golden/functions.revl).
fn name(row: Str) -> Str { return row }

fn make_row(id: Int, name: Str) -> Str {
  return name.concat("/").concat(id.to_str())
}

fn rename(row: Str) -> Str {
  let name = row
  return make_row(1, name)
}

// The same coincidence through a METHOD call. `length` is a module fn and a
// local, and `xs.length()` names the List surface rather than either of them —
// a method call is not a bare identifier, so it can never be the ambiguous
// position, and a reader that fired on every `ident (` would refuse this.
fn length(k: Str) -> Str { return k }

fn read_through(xs: List[Int], k: Str) -> Int {
  let length = k
  return xs.length() + length.length()
}

// A module callable CALLED in a body that binds nothing of its name: the other
// half of the pair, and the overwhelmingly common one.
fn total(xs: List[Int]) -> Int { return xs.length() }

fn count(xs: List[Int]) -> Int { return total(xs) }

// The `=>` fence in `csh_refusal`. A match ARM head is an identifier followed
// by `(`, which is exactly what a bare call looks like to a token reader, so a
// scan without the fence would read `Some(v)` as a call to a module callable
// and refuse this document on the coincidence of the binding below.
fn Wrap(n: Int) -> Int { return n }

fn unwrap_or(o: Opt[Int]) -> Int {
  let Wrap = 1
  return match o { Some(v) => v, None => Wrap }
}

service Cache { fn set(key: Str)  fn put(key: Str)  fn tag(key: Str) -> Str }

// `_check_rebind`'s accepting side: every binding below is legal, and each one
// stands in front of a different way the rule could be widened into refusing it.
//   * `slot` is bound afresh in `set` and again in `put` — the reference clears
//     its local map between methods, so a scope carried across the whole
//     component would refuse the second one;
//   * `slot` is bound inside an `if` arm in `tag` and again after it, where the
//     arm's binding is already out of scope;
//   * `store` is a component-scope name these methods only READ. Rebinding it
//     is the collision the gate does decide, and reading it must stay free.
component C provides cache: Cache {
  let store = effect Map.new() undo store.drop()
  provide cache {
    fn set(key) {
      let slot = key
      effect store.insert(slot, "v")
      undo   store.remove(slot)
    }
    fn put(key) {
      let slot = key
      effect store.insert(slot, "w")
      undo   store.remove(slot)
    }
    fn tag(key) -> Str {
      if (key == "a") {
        let slot = key.concat("-a")
        return slot
      }
      let slot = key.concat("-z")
      return slot
    }
  }
}
